two planes collide at the north las  vegas airport killing all four people  involved this all happening at about 12  noon on sunday while the ntsb and the  faa continue their investigation we're  now learning more about one of the  victims madison kimbrough has more four  people dead sunday afternoon after two  small single engine planes crashed while  one of them was preparing to land at  north las vegas airport and we now have  learned that one of those four was  47-year-old zach rainey the father of  two was learning to become a pilot and  flying with a flight instructor at the  time of the crash a gofundme page was  set up monday afternoon in rainey's  honor to help his family with funeral  expenses while it's still unclear which  plane he was in the federal aviation  administration confirming that a single  engine piper pa-46 and a single-engine  cessna 172 collided on the runway the  piper crashed into a field and the  cessna caught fire and fell into a water  retention pond people who live in the  area near north las vegas airport say  this crash furthers their concern for  their own safety as planes are  constantly flying by throughout the day  the smaller ones are a little more  dangerous because i don't think they  have as much supervision over them for  the maintenance and stuff  and you don't know if the pilot's not  experienced or not according to the  aviation safety network the cessna 172  is registered to benner enterprises llc  which is registered in henderson in a  statement released by matthew benner the  president of air work las vegas he says  quote our thoughts and prayers are with  the families of those involved in  yesterday's terrible accident this world  lost some great people and aviators we  are currently cooperating with the faa  and ntsb to help them with their  investigation
 

